subset: human_subset
subset: mouse_subset
synonym: "crista sagittalis externa" EXACT []
synonym: "sagittal crest" BROAD []
synonym: "sagittal ridge" RELATED []
xref: Wikipedia:Sagittal_crest
is_a: UBERON:0005913 ! zone of bone organ
relationship: part_of UBERON:0003113 ! dermatocranium
property_value: depiction "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Paranthropus_aethiopicus.JPG" xsd:anyURI
property_value: external_ontology_notes "Compare with: AAO:0000384 parasagittal crest (UBERON:0013402)" xsd:string {external_ontology="AAO"}
property_value: function_notes "The sagittal crest serves primarily for attachment of the temporalis muscle, which is one of the main chewing muscles" xsd:string
property_value: taxon_notes "Skulls of some dinosaur species, including tyrannosaurs, possessed well developed sagittal crests. Among mammals, dogs, cats, lions, and many other carnivores have sagittal crests, as do some leaf eaters, including tapirs and some apes. Sagittal crests are found in robust great apes, and some early hominins. Prominent sagittal crests are found among male gorillas and orangutans, and do occur but only rarely in male chimpanzees such as Bili Apes" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0013448
name: sagittal keel
def: "The Sagittal keel (torus) is a thickening of bone on part or all of the midline of the frontal bone, or parietal bones where they meet along the sagittal suture, or on both bones.." [Wikipedia:Sagittal_keel]
subset: human_subset
subset: mouse_subset
synonym: "sagittal torus" RELATED []
xref: Wikipedia:Sagittal_keel
is_a: UBERON:0005913 ! zone of bone organ
relationship: part_of UBERON:0003113 ! dermatocranium
property_value: taxon_notes "Sagittal keels occur in Homo erectus and occasionally Homo heidelbergensis, where they probably served as an armour against shock to the roof of the skull, and as the attachment point for the temporalis muscles. Most Homo sapiens lost them likely as part of the general trend toward thinning of the cranial bones during the Pleistocene, to make room for larger brains. However there is a very small portion of modern humans who have this, but its function and etiology are unknown. Patrick Stewart of Star Trek and the martial artist Shi Yan Ming present good examples of modern humans with this feature." xsd:string {source="Wikipedia:Occipital_bun", taxon="Homo erectus"}

