[Term]
id: UBERON:8930194
name: outer periarterial lymphatic sheath of spleen
def: "The peripheral region of the periarterial lymphatic sheath of the spleen, surrounding the inner periarterial lymphatic sheath of spleen, in which reticulum cells are arranged in cylindrical shells. It contains both T and B lymphocytes and is a site of T-B cell interaction and of antigen-induced B cell proliferation and early plasmablast development, from where plasmablasts migrate towards the red pulp." [DOI:10.1007/BF00217084, DOI:10.1007/BF00221456, DOI:10.1007/BF00225103]
comment: Inner/outer PALS subdivision is described in rat and mouse (Veerman & van Ewijk 1975, doi:10.1007/BF00225103). In adult human spleen the T-cell regions are discontinuous and lack rodent-type PALS architecture (van Krieken et al. 1985, doi:10.1111/j.1365-2559.1985.tb02841.x; Steiniger, Rüttinger & Barth 2003, doi:10.1177/002215540305100511); no inner/outer subdivision has been demonstrated in humans.
subset: added_by_HRA
subset: human_subset
subset: mouse_subset
